1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:02:16,380
Here for 43 years I've seen regulation after regulation come along thinking oh boy here comes somebody else that's going to be watching everything we do.

2
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:20,380
A lot of people are concerned with what it'll do to certain property rights.

3
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:29,380
Nobody really knows what to expect. What's this process really going to be about? Can we trust these feds and is this going to be a good program?

4
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:33,380
And there's a lot of sort of suspicion and feeling out and rightly so.

5
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:36,380
Initially I had some concerns as well and talked to a lot of people.

6
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:46,380
One of the reasons I really perked up when I heard about this is I wanted to make sure that this wasn't another layer of bureaucracy that I'd need to deal with for what I do on our farm.

7
00:02:46,380 --> 00:02:57,380
Farmers have concerns about whether or not they can continue to bring their tractor across the river and Somalbia organizations have talked about whether or not they'll be able to put a bridge across the river in a new location.

8
00:02:57,380 --> 00:03:03,380
It didn't take me very long once I got involved to realize that's not what this is about.

9
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:11,380
The committee overall over the three years did a great job of addressing all those concerns and addressing them in the management plan.

10
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:15,380
This is not about adding more regulation.

11
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:19,380
I managed the Wild and Scenic Rivers program in New England for the National Park Service.

12
00:03:19,380 --> 00:03:24,380
Back in the 50s and 60s was an era of big dam projects.

13
00:03:24,380 --> 00:03:35,380
I think some of the folks in Congress felt like there ought to be a complementary way to preserve certain rivers in their free-flowing condition rather than trying to develop them for other purposes.

14
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:51,460
What's happening on the Mississippi and on other rivers in New England is pretty unique and pretty rare.

15
00:03:51,460 --> 00:03:59,460
People coming together to try to protect these rivers through the Wild and Scenic Rivers program without creating a federal river park.

16
00:03:59,460 --> 00:04:06,460
Local people sought through their congressmen to nominate the river as a candidate Wild and Scenic River.

17
00:04:06,460 --> 00:04:09,460
The first test is, is it a free-flowing river?

18
00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:14,460
The second test would be, is there something special about the place?

19
00:04:14,460 --> 00:04:20,460
I think for the Mrs. Coy it was pretty apparent that there was some special values to us right away.

20
00:04:20,460 --> 00:04:25,460
And really the third test that we take very seriously is community empowerment.

21
00:04:25,460 --> 00:04:34,460
I think there was some concern that, you know, since this is a federal designation that the government was going to take over control of the development and activity along the river.

22
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:43,460
But since it really included only federal permits, I think people at the end of the days saw the utility of the designation, so it passed pretty quickly.

23
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:46,460
We are an invited guest and we have to behave that way.

24
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:51,460
We try to place ourselves in a position of supporting the local communities, the local partners,

25
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:55,460
and providing good technical support, good advice and funding support.

26
00:04:55,460 --> 00:05:00,460
We don't have any ability or desire to control or dictate anything.

27
00:05:00,460 --> 00:05:09,460
At the end of the day there was zero government interference in the rivers being preserved and being used and it makes me actually feel good about our government.

28
00:05:09,460 --> 00:05:15,050
Especially this day and age.

29
00:05:15,050 --> 00:05:18,050
You know, sometimes when you live so close to something you don't really see it.

30
00:05:18,050 --> 00:05:22,050
It's there and you think about it some, but you don't really see it in a way.

31
00:05:22,050 --> 00:05:29,050
It's a hidden resource that a lot of people just drive over and don't even think about until they see a kayak or a canoe out there and say, wait a second.

32
00:05:29,050 --> 00:05:34,050
There's something different out there and I can take my kids and family out there and enjoy the river.

33
00:05:34,050 --> 00:05:47,050
If you're a landowner that owns land next to the river, I would think that you would be excited about supporting this because it's celebrating something that you already own.

34
00:05:47,050 --> 00:05:56,050
You should take pride in the fact that the center of your town life, the Mrs. Goy River, is being recognized nationally as a wild and scenic river.

35
00:05:56,050 --> 00:06:00,050
Mrs. Goy is a very special place, I think.

36
00:06:00,050 --> 00:06:07,050
If the river were not to be protected or were much worse shaped, the value of our house would go down.

37
00:06:07,050 --> 00:06:10,050
The value of our life, the quality of our life would go down.

38
00:06:10,050 --> 00:06:16,050
The designation does typically come with some federal funding support to help implement the management strategy.

39
00:06:16,050 --> 00:06:21,050
Some money has come back for education and conservation commission projects.

40
00:06:21,050 --> 00:06:28,050
Potential access points along the river that may be partially paid for by the wild and scenic river program.

41
00:06:28,050 --> 00:06:33,050
If it entices someone to come here and spend some of their dollars, there's certainly a value there.

42
00:06:33,050 --> 00:06:45,620
I think people just in their minds say whatever we did is the right thing because it seems to be working and people seem to be getting out and enjoying the river.

43
00:06:45,620 --> 00:06:53,620
Looking at the benefits, the pros and cons, and coming up with your own decision as to whether or not that's something you'd like to see.

44
00:06:53,620 --> 00:06:57,620
Most of the issues we talk about at town meeting are going to cost us something.

45
00:06:57,620 --> 00:07:01,620
We're not asking the people at the town meetings for any money whatsoever.

46
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:09,620
I do think it's an opportunity. In order to be seized and really made something out of by the communities, they've got to see the opportunity as well.

47
00:07:09,620 --> 00:07:15,620
Before the designation, I think the communities were connected via the roads and highways.

48
00:07:15,620 --> 00:07:21,620
But at the designation, the communities became connected to the river understanding that the river connects us together.

49
00:07:21,620 --> 00:07:26,620
So if you respect the community you live in, the designation is not going to hurt you.

50
00:07:26,620 --> 00:07:29,620
And at the end of the day, you're going to benefit your children and grandchildren.

51
00:07:29,620 --> 00:07:36,620
So it's definitely something that I would hardly endorse and it hasn't affected the operation of Sudbury government in any manner.

